theory.intro:
A function has an inverse function only when it is **one-to-one**, that is, when it passes the horizontal line test. If it is not, restrict its domain to a piece on which the function is always increasing or always decreasing.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-3.0,0) -- (4.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.0) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (0.6,-0.6) -- (0.6,4.4);
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-1.8:3.0, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.55*(\x - 0.6)*(\x - 0.6) + 0.8});
\fill (0.6,0.8) circle (2.5pt);
\node at (1.6,0.5) {$(h,k)$};
\node at (-2.0,1.6) {$x \le h$};
\node at (2.9,1.6) {$x \ge h$};
\node at (1.6,4.2) {$x = h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
The quadratic function $f(x) = (x - 4)^2 + 1$ is many-to-one. State two maximal domain restrictions so that the inverse is a function.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.75]
\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (8.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.8) -- (0,7.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=1.55:6.45, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {(\x - 4)*(\x - 4) + 1});
\draw[dashed] (0.5,5) -- (7.5,5);
\fill (4,1) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$(4,1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
The cubic polynomial $f(x) = x^3 - 3x$ has stationary points at $x = -1$ and $x = 1$. Find the maximal domain containing $x = 0$ on which $f(x)$ is one-to-one.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (-2.7,0) -- (2.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.8) -- (0,3.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.15:2.15, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {\x*\x*\x - 3*\x});
\fill (-1,2) circle (2.5pt) node[above left] {$(-1,2)$};
\fill (1,-2) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$(1,-2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
